1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,519
Caches and services only help if clients
can depend on your HTTP contract for
years. APIs are long-term contracts —
idempotency and errors matter as much as
happy paths.

2
00:00:12,639 --> 00:00:18,559
A beautiful endpoint that double-charges
on retry is not beautiful. Resource
modeling comes first.

3
00:00:18,679 --> 00:00:41,399
Prefer nouns and HTTP verbs that match:
POST /orders creates, GET /orders/{id}
reads, POST /orders/{id}/cancel may be
justified when cancel is a real action.
Prefer /users/{id}/orders over
/getUserOrders.

4
00:00:41,519 --> 00:00:48,519
An endpoint named /doCreateOrder that
always returns 200 is a remote procedure
call wearing a thin disguise.

5
00:00:48,639 --> 00:01:02,879
Breaking changes casually — renaming
fields, changing meaning, removing
properties without versioning — punish
every client. Prefer additive evolution
when you can; reserve hard versions for
true breaks.

6
00:01:02,999 --> 00:01:17,000
Deprecate before removal. Look at this
java for a moment. Validation at the edge
keeps the domain cleaner. Consistent error
shapes let clients automate handling —
code, message, fields, traceId.

7
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:31,639
Clients branch on code; humans read
message; support uses traceId. Changing
that shape later is itself a breaking
change. Prefer problem-details or a
documented JSON envelope over free-text.

8
00:01:31,759 --> 00:01:43,879
Recycling an error code to mean something
new is a silent break. Pagination and
filtering prevent "return the entire
table" accidents. Cursor pagination often
ages better than deep offset pages.

9
00:01:43,999 --> 00:01:53,999
State max page size server-side; clients
asking for a million rows should fail
loudly. Filter parameters should be
documented, capped, and preferably
indexed.

10
00:01:54,119 --> 00:02:06,639
Idempotency keys on POSTs that matter mean
a client can retry safely after a timeout
without creating two orders. Why
idempotency? Clients retry; servers must
not double-apply side effects.

11
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:20,159
Walk a payment POST: client sends
Idempotency-Key; server charges once and
stores the response; network drops; client
retries with the same key; server returns
the stored response without charging
again.

12
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:35,560
Without TTL, the idempotency table grows
forever. Without uniqueness scoped
correctly, different users collide. Money,
bookings, and side-effecting POSTs earn
this machinery; pure GETs do not.

13
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:42,240
Document keys in public API docs with
examples — server support without client
education is half a solution.

14
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:52,760
Leaky entities as DTOs couple clients to
persistence and leak lazy-loading problems
from Episode Seventy-Five into public
JSON. Keep entities inward.

15
00:02:52,880 --> 00:03:07,320
Public API docs should show happy paths
and failure paths side by side — an
OpenAPI file that only lists 200 responses
lies by omission. Include validation
failures, auth failures, and idempotent
replays.

16
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:18,080
Mature POST behavior: validate, apply
once, return consistent bodies on replay,
and never return 500 with a half-applied
side effect without a recovery story.

17
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:28,280
Status codes are part of the contract: 201
or 200 on success, 409 when a key reuses
different bodies, 400 when validation
fails before charge.

18
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:39,439
Breaking changes casually — renaming
amount fields from cents to dollars
without versioning — cause real financial
events. Treat API changes with the
seriousness of schema migrations.

19
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:46,960
Interview answers should stress contracts,
idempotency, and error consistency — not
only annotation trivia.

20
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:57,000
When APIs need to fan work out
asynchronously, events enter — and with
them a new failure mode: at-least-once
delivery without double-applying side
effects.
